{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "The harsh morning light stabbed through the gap in the curtains, but something felt…off. Arthur blinked, the familiar fuzziness of sleep receding, replaced by a jarring clarity. He sat up in bed, and the wood of the headboard felt strangely…smooth, almost manufactured. He glanced around his room, noticing the overly-realistic textures of the wallpaper, the perfect symmetry of the furniture. A wave of dizziness washed over him, and he reached out to touch the bedside table. It wasn't wood, not exactly. It was a composite material, flawlessly mimicking the grain. He tried to rationalize it – a bizarre dream, a vivid hallucination – but the unsettling feeling persisted, a cold dread creeping into his chest. He looked at his reflection in the mirror, and the face staring back was undeniably *him*, yet subtly…perfect. No blemishes, no wrinkles, just an unnervingly flawless replica.\n\n***\n\nHe stumbled out of bed, the floor feeling strangely springy beneath his feet. The hallway was identical to his own, down to the perfectly placed potted plant. A notification popped up in his vision – a translucent overlay shimmering in the air – displaying a character menu. \"Arthur Penhaligon,\" it read. \"Level 1. Class: Commoner. Stats: Strength 10, Dexterity 8, Intelligence 12.\" Panic seized him. This wasn't his life. This wasn’t *real*. He tentatively poked at the menu, scrolling through options: skills, inventory, quests. He felt a sickening lurch as he realized the horrifying truth: he was inside a video game. The world around him wasn't just rendered; it was *code*. He was a character in someone else’s meticulously crafted reality, trapped within its digital confines, and the game was just beginning.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "The", "logprob": -0.6412928104400635}, {"token": "▁harsh", "logprob": -1.4735186100006104}, {"token": "▁morning", "logprob": -1.4890